{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And his minister Joshua the son of Nun, a young man</b>: Ibn Ezra wrote (on Exodus 33:11) that Joshua lived one hundred and ten years (Joshua 24:29), and the Sages said (Zevaḥim 118b) that he conquered [the land] for seven years and divided it for seven years; if so, he was fifty-six years old, and how would Scripture call him \"a young man\"? Thus his explanation is: <b>and his minister Joshua the son of Nun</b> — the service of a young man. But in my opinion, it is the way of the sacred language that any attendant is called \"נַעַר\" (na'ar, 'young man' / 'attendant'), for the dignitary is the man, and the one who attends him is called \"נַעַר\". And thus: \"Gehazi, נַעֲרוֹ (his attendant)\" (II Kings 4:12); \"Let the נְעָרִים (young men/attendants) arise and play before us\" (II Samuel 2:14); and likewise \"ten נְעָרִים carrying Joab's weapons\" (ibid. 18:15), for Joab would entrust his weapons only to mighty men of valor who stood by him. And it is written (Joshua 6:22), \"And to the two men, the spies, Joshua said,\" and it is written (in the following verse), \"And the נְעָרִים, the spies, came\" and so on — because they were attendants of the congregation, spying for them. And there are many similar cases, and thus: \"the king's נַעֲרֵי (attendants), his ministers\" (Esther 2:2), clarifying that they are the men who stand before the king, ministers of his person, not ministers of the court. If so, the verse says: and his minister Joshua the son of Nun — a constant attendant, who would not depart from within the Tent." "The grammatical form of <b>בִּן נוּן</b> (bin nun) — it comes with a ḥiriq in place of a segol, and likewise: \"The words of Agur בִּן (son of) Jakeh\" (Proverbs 30:1); \"that was בִּן (a son of) the night and בִּן (a son of) the night perished\" (Jonah 4:10); \"if בִּן (the son of) striking the wicked\" (Deuteronomy 25:2). And despite all this, I marvel why not one [instance] has been preserved in the name of this righteous man that would come according to the normal practice [of vocalization]. And I think that they were accustomed to call him thus out of honor, for he was the greatest among the disciples of Moses our Master, and they would call him בִּינוּן (binun), meaning the discerning one, for there was none as discerning and wise as he. Or its meaning is: Joshua, whom understanding begot — making נוּן (nun) from the language of \"before the sun his name יִנּוֹן (shall continue)\" (Psalms 72:17)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {1 "The phrase 'according to the normal practice' refers to the expected plural form בְּנֵי (with segol) rather than the anomalous singular בִּן (with ḥiriq); Ramban questions why this peculiar form is consistently used for Joshua."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:51.344481143Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}